WEBVTT

00:00:00.210 --> 00:00:01.750
Short and sweet number 11.

00:00:02.050 --> 00:00:12.070
The Shulchan Aruch, as understood by the Gra, says that it is forbidden to drink any alcoholic or prestigious non-alcoholic beverages in a non-Jewish establishment.

00:00:12.510 --> 00:00:19.210
Now this Gezerah we discussed was a later Gezerah than the Isser of B'shalakum and Pasakum,

00:00:19.290 --> 00:00:24.250
and therefore it is not subject to the same level of restrictions as those previous Gezerahs.

00:00:24.510 --> 00:00:29.510
One exception would be that if the drink was removed from the non-Jews domain,

00:00:29.510 --> 00:00:33.550
Again, assuming it's a kosher beverage, then it would be allowed.

00:00:33.770 --> 00:00:35.430
Now, how far does it have to be removed?

00:00:35.490 --> 00:00:44.450
The Gra, Paschan, that the beverage must be brought into the Jew's domain, the Rishus, whether that's their home or a hotel room, it has to be that far removed.

00:00:44.670 --> 00:00:48.390
However, the Darken Tshuva says that it only needs to be taken out of the non-Jew's domain.

00:00:48.470 --> 00:00:51.170
It doesn't have to be brought all the way to the Rishus of the Jew.

00:00:51.530 --> 00:00:58.190
We did say that everyone agrees that if it's a designated outdoor eating area of a non-Jewish bar, that's still considered inside of the non-Jew's domain.

00:00:58.190 --> 00:01:02.010
The Shach told us that this prohibition does have an exception.

00:01:02.150 --> 00:01:09.010
In a case where it would cause animosity not to drink in this establishment, then it would be permissible to drink.

00:01:09.130 --> 00:01:16.630
As well as we mentioned, another exception is if it's a sporadic and inconsistent manner of drinking, it would be permitted.

00:01:16.830 --> 00:01:21.490
So the example we said was if a person does not sit down, they're just walking around with a drink, and this is not frequent,

00:01:21.590 --> 00:01:27.610
this is a one-time or very infrequent occurrence, then it would be permissible.

00:01:27.610 --> 00:01:33.990
The Huchas Katanos, the Kaf HaChaim, actually said that the Shulchan Aruch holds that non-alcoholic beverages are permitted.

00:01:34.150 --> 00:01:43.030
He only forbids alcoholic drinks, but the Groth said that the Shulchan Aruch held even non-alcoholic drinks are problematic if they are prestigious.

00:01:43.290 --> 00:01:48.770
We have the opinion of the Ramah, who held that only date beer is prohibited in a non-Jewish establishment.

00:01:49.030 --> 00:01:52.990
Date beer was what was drunk in the times of the Gemara, and that was the only thing that was prohibited.

00:01:52.990 --> 00:01:58.330
All other beverages, according to the Rama, would be permitted in a non-Jewish establishment or a non-Jewish bar.

00:01:58.550 --> 00:02:06.250
The question of conducting a business meeting over a beer at a non-Jewish bar, we said, is a machukes or Moshe Feinstein held.

00:02:06.310 --> 00:02:16.290
It would be allowed because we'd have to avoid causing any animosity with the non-Jewish employee or boss or co-worker.

00:02:16.430 --> 00:02:18.810
In a situation where there's no animosity, it would not be allowed.

00:02:18.810 --> 00:02:23.670
But to avoid it, avoid animosity, we will allow it.

00:02:23.710 --> 00:02:25.710
The Orletzion, however, said it would be forbidden.

00:02:26.350 --> 00:02:31.410
However, he did say that if it was non-alcoholic drinks that were being drunk at the bar, it would be permissible.

00:02:31.630 --> 00:02:38.390
We then even discussed the question of drinking coffee, which is a prestigious drink, albeit not alcoholic, but it is a prestigious drink.

00:02:38.510 --> 00:02:41.390
Can a person drink a coffee at a non-Jewish-owned coffee shop?

00:02:41.590 --> 00:02:44.590
So the Shulchan Aruch, as understood by the Gra, would say that it's forbidden.

00:02:44.590 --> 00:02:49.570
We said that the Halachas Kitanos and the Kaf HaChaim would say it's permitted because the drink is non-alcoholic.

00:02:49.630 --> 00:02:56.270
The Rama, we said, would be subject to a Machlokas, the Pischei Teshuva, Chochmas Adam, even the Vada Arbarotzo is a very early source,

00:02:56.430 --> 00:03:00.990
say that in the Rama, even according to the Rama, it would be forbidden to drink the coffee.

00:03:01.090 --> 00:03:06.690
However, Rehershal Schachter did tell us that even though one should follow this Chochmas Adam,

00:03:06.850 --> 00:03:11.770
if it's not common to socialize with strangers at a coffee shop, there's room to be lenient.

00:03:11.770 --> 00:03:15.610
Okay, then we discussed the question of attending a non-Jewish party.

00:03:15.810 --> 00:03:18.650
So, again, this party is being arranged by non-Jews.

00:03:18.690 --> 00:03:23.730
So, in a situation where it's being arranged by non-Jews and the majority of attendees are non-Jewish,

00:03:23.770 --> 00:03:28.450
Rav Moshe Feinstein said it would be forbidden to be at that party.

00:03:30.330 --> 00:03:36.230
However, the one exception Rav Moshe said is if there would be animosity,

00:03:36.390 --> 00:03:39.270
it would be permitted to attend and even to drink an alcoholic drink.

00:03:39.270 --> 00:03:46.930
The Orletzion held that it would be permitted to attend this party, but not to drink alcohol, even in a situation of animosity.

00:03:47.070 --> 00:03:51.670
So that would be the Machlokas subject between the Rav Moshe and the Orletzion.

00:03:51.850 --> 00:03:56.890
We did mention, though, if the party is being organized by non-Jews, but the majority of attendees are Jewish,

00:03:57.070 --> 00:04:01.350
so then we said everyone would agree that it would be permitted to attend and even to drink alcohol.

00:04:01.710 --> 00:04:05.390
The question of attending a non-Jewish wedding feast was Machlokas, the Taz said,

00:04:05.390 --> 00:04:09.250
It's an Issa Dar Esa, and you're not allowed to attend, even if it's going to cause animosity, you cannot go.

00:04:09.410 --> 00:04:14.490
The Shach, however, said it's only an Issa Midr HaBanan, and therefore, if it will cause animosity to not attend, it would be permitted.

00:04:14.750 --> 00:04:19.350
The Sefer B'ni Yisroel Amin said that if the Jews have their own table, it would be permissible as well.

00:04:19.530 --> 00:04:27.230
And we said if a Ger wanted to attend their parents' birthday party, or even a regular meal of their parents, it would be permissible,

00:04:28.110 --> 00:04:30.410
even though there are non-Jews present, because they are Ger.

00:04:30.410 --> 00:04:38.530
But we said it would be permissible for a couple of factors, including the fact that mitzvah keep it of an aim, and that would be permissible as well.
